WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.838
This exercise gives you a nearly complete INSERT
statement Your job is

00:00:04.862 --> 00:00:09.125
to provide one missing value We want to add Sarah
Adams age

00:00:09.138 --> 00:00:15.012
twenty-eight gender Female The only missing field is
the MRN Because

00:00:15.038 --> 00:00:18.400
the MRN column is intended to be unique the new
value should

00:00:18.425 --> 00:00:23.025
not duplicate an MRN that already exists Replace the
blank with a

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:27.600
quoted value such as MRN one zero one one In SQL a

00:00:27.625 --> 00:00:32.325
text value belongs inside single quotes Before you
run it check the

00:00:32.338 --> 00:00:38.000
structure The INSERT column list contains MRN full
name age and gender

00:00:38.888 --> 00:00:43.412
The VALUES section must contain four values in that
same order Then

00:00:43.438 --> 00:00:48.262
RETURNING asks PostgreSQL to show the new row after
the insert Run

00:00:48.288 --> 00:00:53.600
the statement and check the returned values If
PostgreSQL reports a uniqueness

00:00:53.625 --> 00:00:58.312
error that is useful information It means the
database is enforcing the

00:00:58.338 --> 00:01:03.362
rule that the MRN cannot be duplicated Choose
another unique MRN and

00:01:03.388 --> 00:01:07.600
try again This is a good example of how database
constraints and

00:01:07.625 --> 00:01:12.162
SQL statements work together Your SQL asks to add a
row but

00:01:12.188 --> 00:01:15.650
the schema still decides whether that row satisfies
the rules defined for

00:01:15.662 --> 00:01:16.200
the table
